Use shifts and masks for sector and cluster sizes.

Add an argument to write, read that causes the data portion of the
I/O to bypass the cache.  Add the same to blockdev.  For the cache,
a write should write to disk and evict the sector. A read should
fill it from the cache if present, otherwise return it from the disk
but not add it to the cache.

Add a function to BlockDev to return the size of the device/partition.

Refactor test_fat32 a bit so it can be built for firmware (with a
suitable block device, like an SD or CF card and driver) and run on a
target platform.

Investigate cluster loading; when loading a sector ask the cache to
fill the entire cluster.  Add a fill() method to BlockDev for this.

The data structures used all have a little-endian dependency. Is this
even worth the bother these days?  Maybe look into it if it becomes an
issue, as at that point there will be a BE system to test it on.

Consider adding 8-bit ASCII compatible LFNs and simply use '=' for
anything unprintable.  This could have a simply interface that
explicitly adds an LFN for an SFN, removes an LFN for an SFN, renames
an SFN's LFN, or looks up the SFN for an LFN.  This way systems can
omit the LFN support pretty easily as they're entirely separate.
